the tears of a mother worried for her  only child and alone since he was  detained by police nearly eight months  ago  Atika burgum says faisal had gone to the  pharmacy to buy her medicine  when police detained him under special  legislation the police files his faisal  who worked in a shop selling water  purifiers in indian-administered Kashmir  was involved in separatist activities  but why does anybody know if this is not  oppression then what is what have we  done to deserve this  why does a poor widow like me have to go  through such difficult times why am i  subject to so much tyranny  I've been appealing to authorities for  several months to look at this case  twenty-two-year-old faisal is one of  more than seven thousand Kashmiri  detained by the Indian government since  it revoked the Regents autonomy last  year New Delhi has said the detentions  are necessary to maintain order and  prevent violence it now says it's  looking into releasing some of the  prisoners after the Supreme Court asked  the government to prevent the spread of  coronavirus and prisons human rights  groups say the detentions without charge  or trial violate both Indian and  international law and with many courts  now closed the public health emergency  should not be used to bypass  accountability many of them have been  begin simply under you know Wabble  orders and that is the most shocking  part actually there is no written even  document so the chances of being  released of those detainees are  extremely problematic extremely critical  the government has closed all schools in  the disputed Kashmir region they  reopened in February only to be shut  again two weeks later after the first  cases of coronavirus were reported in  the territory battalion party it  definitely has a negative impact on the  children's studies although some schools  in tutorials - but the government didn't  do anything for children studying in  government schools there was also a  complete communications blackout with  landline mobile and Internet services  cut off for months slow speed 2g  internet was only restored last month  but 4G is still  unavailable rights groups are calling  for the full restoration of Internet  services so that people have access to  health information and their loved ones  during the pandemic  I am suffering I want to see him once  but I can't afford to I don't know what  to do many who can afford to have met  their relatives but I can't because I'm  poor I'm struggling just to live how can  I even think of going to meet him Atika  has had one video call with faisal whose  friends are giving him money to get by  but the lockdown means they can no  longer visit her Elisabeth puranam  al-jazeera New Delhi
 

